===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be firmly [[fixed]] in a specified [[position]].
#: {{ux|en|The bullet missed its target and '''lodged''' in the bark of a tree.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To firmly fix in a specified position.
#: {{ux|en|I've got some spinach '''lodged''' between my teeth.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To stay in a [[boarding-house]], [[pay]]ing [[rent]] to the [[resident]] [[landlord]] or [[landlady]].
#: {{ux|en|The detective Sherlock Holmes '''lodged''' in Baker Street.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To stay in any place or shelter.
#: {{syn|en|stay over|stop|Thesaurus:sojourn}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=I|scene=i|passage=Stay and '''lodge''' by me this night.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Comus|passage=Something holy '''lodges''' in that breast.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To drive (an animal) to [[covert]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1819|author=John Mayer|title=The Sportsman's Directory, or Park and Gamekeeper's Companion
|passage=This is the time that the horseman are flung out, not having the cry to lead them to the death. When quadruped animals of the venery or hunting kind are at rest, the stag is said to be harboured, the buck '''lodged''', the fox kennelled, the badger earthed, the otter vented or watched, the hare formed, and the rabbit set. When you find and rouse up the stag and buck, they are said to be imprimed: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[supply]] with a [[room]] or [[place]] to [[sleep]] in for a time.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put [[money]], [[jewellery]], or other [[valuable]]s away for [[safety]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|chiefly|legal|politics}} To [[place]] (a statement, etc.) with the [[proper]] [[authority|authorities]] (such as courts, etc.).
#: {{co|en|to '''lodge''' a complaint}}
#: {{ux|en|Powers that are '''lodge'''d with the cabinet.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|work=BBC Sport|author=Phil McNulty|date=14 October 2020|title=England 0-1 Denmark: 'Harry Maguire looked devoid of confidence in Nations League loss'|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54549087|passage=He [Harry Maguire] maintains his innocence and has '''lodged''' an appeal—which means a retrial and the conviction being set aside in the meantime—{{...|but it feels and looks like the weight of this incident and its public aftermath has carried over into the new season}}.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260306030358/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/54549087|archivedate=6 March 2026}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Jessica Elgot|title=MPs move to lodge parliamentary motion to strip Prince Andrew of dukedom|date=2025-10-21|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/oct/21/mps-move-to-lodge-parliamentary-motion-to-strip-prince-andrew-of-dukedom|passage=MPs have moved to '''lodge''' a parliamentary motion to strip Prince Andrew of his dukedom, in a rarely permitted move in the Commons. {{...}}  On Tuesday, the SNP '''lodged''' an early-day motion (EDM) to formally remove Prince Andrew’s title as Duke of York, which he has said he will no longer use.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261018020830/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/oct/21/mps-move-to-lodge-parliamentary-motion-to-strip-prince-andrew-of-dukedom|archivedate=18 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become flattened, as grass or grain, when overgrown or beaten down by the wind.
#: {{ux|en|The heavy rain caused the wheat to '''lodge'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to flatten, as grass or grain.

